toricMarkov -- calculates a generating set of the toric ideal I_A, given A; invokes "markov" from 4ti2
******************************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        toricMarkov(A) or toricMarkov(A, InputType => "lattice") or toricMarkov(A,R)
  * Inputs:
      * A, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, whose columns parametrize the
        toric variety; the toric ideal is the kernel of the map defined by A.
        Otherwise, if InputType is set to "lattice", the rows of A are a
        lattice basis and the toric ideal is the saturation of the lattice
        basis ideal.
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, polynomial ring in which the
        toric ideal $I_A$ should live
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * InputType => a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value null,
        which is the string "lattice" if rows of A specify a lattice basis
      * Precision => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ, or a *note string:
        (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value 64, 32, 64, or "arbitrary", the
        precision of the integers used during the computation
  * Outputs:
      * B, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, whose rows form a Markov
        Basis of the lattice $\{z : z \text{ is integral and } A z = 0\}$ or
        the lattice spanned by the rows of A if the option InputType =>
        "lattice" is used

Description
===========

Suppose we would like to comput the toric ideal defining the variety
parametrized by the following matrix:

+--------------------------------+
|i1 : A = matrix"1,1,1,1;0,1,2,3"|
|                                |
|o1 = | 1 1 1 1 |                |
|     | 0 1 2 3 |                |
|                                |
|              2       4         |
|o1 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ          |
+--------------------------------+

Since there are 4 columns, the ideal will live in the polynomial ring with 4
variables.

+-----------------------+
|i2 : R = QQ[a..d]      |
|                       |
|o2 = R                 |
|                       |
|o2 : PolynomialRing    |
+-----------------------+
|i3 : M = toricMarkov(A)|
|                       |
|o3 = | 0 1  -2 1 |     |
|     | 1 -2 1  0 |     |
|     | 1 -1 -1 1 |     |
|                       |
|              3       4|
|o3 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ |
+-----------------------+

Note that rows of M are the exponents of minimal generators of $I_A$.  To get
the ideal, we can do the following:

+------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : I = toBinomial(M,R)                        |
|                                                |
|               2           2                    |
|o4 = ideal (- c  + b*d, - b  + a*c, - b*c + a*d)|
|                                                |
|o4 : Ideal of R                                 |
+------------------------------------------------+

Alternately, we might wish to give a lattice basis ideal instead of the matrix
A. The lattice basis will be specified by a matrix, as follows:

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : B = syz A                                                               |
|                                                                             |
|o5 = | -1 2  |                                                               |
|     | 2  -3 |                                                               |
|     | -1 0  |                                                               |
|     | 0  1  |                                                               |
|                                                                             |
|              4       2                                                      |
|o5 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : N = toricMarkov(transpose B, InputType => "lattice")                    |
|                                                                             |
|o6 = | 0 1  -2 1 |                                                           |
|     | 1 -2 1  0 |                                                           |
|     | 1 -1 -1 1 |                                                           |
|                                                                             |
|              3       4                                                      |
|o6 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : J = toBinomial(N,R) -- toricMarkov(transpose B, R, InputType =>         |
|                                                                             |
|               2           2                                                 |
|o7 = ideal (- c  + b*d, - b  + a*c, - b*c + a*d)                             |
|                                                                             |
|o7 : Ideal of R                                                              |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|"lattice")                                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

We can see that the two ideals are equal:

+-----------+
|i8 : I == J|
|           |
|o8 = true  |
+-----------+

Also, notice that instead of the sequence of commands above, we could have used
the following:

+------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : toricMarkov(A,R)                           |
|                                                |
|               2           2                    |
|o9 = ideal (- c  + b*d, - b  + a*c, - b*c + a*d)|
|                                                |
|o9 : Ideal of R                                 |
+------------------------------------------------+

